        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.708,
            0.044,
            0.847,
            0.06
          ],
          "content": "Tissues in Action"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": "N 43"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.09,
            0.084,
            0.162,
            0.137
          ],
          "content": "Q"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.172,
            0.1,
            0.47,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": "Bridging Science and Society"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.137,
            0.745,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": "In nature, plant pathologists have observed a disease in plants called crown gall disease (Fig. 3.20). In this disease, tumour-like swellings develop on the stems due to rapid and uncontrolled cell division. The disease is caused by a bacterium called Agrobacterium tumefaciens."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.202,
            0.745,
            0.296
          ],
          "content": "Instead of only trying to cure this disease, scientists studied how a bacterium transfers its genetic material into plant cells. This knowledge was later used in plant tissue culture and genetic engineering. Today, Agrobacterium is used as a tool to introduce useful genes into plants for the production of valuable phytochemicals, improved crops and disease-resistant varieties."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.762,
            0.128,
            0.881,
            0.243
          ],
          "content": "",
          "captions": [],
          "footnotes": [
            {
              "bbox": [
                0.76,
                0.247,
                0.879,
                0.276
              ],
              "content": "Fig. 3.20: Crown gall disease"
            }
          ],
          "image_source": "images/page_15_image_6.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.169,
            0.336,
            0.302,
            0.354
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "At a Glance"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.371,
            0.756,
            0.406
          ],
          "content": "Tissues are groups of similar cells that work together to perform specific functions."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.408,
            0.756,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": "Different tissues coordinate with one another to perform life processes in plants and animals."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.446,
            0.755,
            0.481
          ],
          "content": "Plant tissues are broadly classified into meristematic and permanent tissues, depending on their ability to divide."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.483,
            0.755,
            0.519
          ],
          "content": "Functionally, plant tissues can be categorised as protecting tissue, supporting tissue and conducting tissue."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.521,
            0.755,
            0.556
          ],
          "content": "Permanent tissues may be simple (made-up of one type of cell) or complex (made-up of more than one type of cell)."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.558,
            0.755,
            0.593
          ],
          "content": "Simple permanent tissues include parenchyma, collenchyma and schlerenchyma."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.596,
            0.756,
            0.631
          ],
          "content": "Complex permanent tissues include xylem and phloem that transport water and food respectively to all parts of the plant."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.633,
            0.755,
            0.668
          ],
          "content": "Animal tissues are mainly of four types; epithelial, connective, muscular and nervous tissues."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.671,
            0.756,
            0.706
          ],
          "content": "Epithelial tissue forms the outer covering of the body or protective lining to internal organs to provide protection and other functions."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.708,
            0.756,
            0.743
          ],
          "content": "Connective tissue connects and supports various organs and tissues of the body."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.746,
            0.755,
            0.781
          ],
          "content": "Muscular tissue produces voluntary and involuntary movements for locomotion, and other movements in the body."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.783,
            0.756,
            0.818
          ],
          "content": "Nervous tissue consists of neurons to receive and transmit impulses, and helps regulate body activities."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.146,
            0.821,
            0.649,
            0.839
          ],
          "content": "Skeletal system protects organs and provides support."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.841,
            0.756,
            0.894
          ],
          "content": "In our body, movement occurs by the coordination of muscles and bones (musculoskeletal system), under the control of the nervous system."
        },
        {
